  "question": {
    "html": "<p>In a Compton scattering experiment, an X-ray photon of initial wavelength <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\lambda_i = 0.050\\ \\text{nm}\"></span> collides with a stationary electron. The photon is scattered at an angle of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"60^\\circ\"></span>.</p><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong> State the Compton wavelength shift formula. [1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> Calculate the change in wavelength <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\Delta \\lambda\"></span> of the photon. Use <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"h = 6.63 \\times 10^{-34}\\ \\text{J s}\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}\\ \\text{kg}\"></span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"c = 3.00 \\times 10^{8}\\ \\text{m s}^{-1}\"></span>. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c\"><p><strong>c</strong> Determine the wavelength <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\lambda_f\"></span> of the scattered photon. [1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"d\"><p><strong>d</strong> Explain how Compton scattering supports the particle nature of light. [1]</p></section>"
